[{:db/id 17592186172005 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e53ee3ed-f667-5b74-b94f-f7c404a83247"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172006 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4f0c3a40-75d0-54f9-9bbc-0aacfac8fedd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172007 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2327e247-df72-5db4-a448-f8b66a015dc8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172008 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b0991a4b-acbc-5194-a13a-e698ad64e9cb" "question-2e0173ce-d070-51b0-81c2-1bf7c8171fcf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e53ee3ed-f667-5b74-b94f-f7c404a83247" :question/id #uuid "e53ee3ed-f667-5b74-b94f-f7c404a83247" :question/math-academy-id "q-96677" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The execution time, in seconds, of a computer program\n\nII. The number of students in a class with blood type $A$\n\nIII. The number of books read by a student in a month" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Executions of a computer program can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time it takes to complete a single execution.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of students is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of books is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4f620f94-aa7b-56b8-b107-dbbda4003603"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4f620f94-aa7b-56b8-b107-dbbda4003603" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f620f94-aa7b-56b8-b107-dbbda4003603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8214dd72-d6d8-590d-81b7-fddc4ba96e98" :answer-field/choices ["answer-34ee353c-18cb-58d7-b73d-63478f3748f9" "answer-2c836109-2926-5149-86de-cfbf47a6956c" "answer-8214dd72-d6d8-590d-81b7-fddc4ba96e98" "answer-fa30ad19-820a-5c33-a201-b90a4102fe1c" "answer-30f9ac07-0f6b-5c34-a6ab-9f12e00a0258"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34ee353c-18cb-58d7-b73d-63478f3748f9" :answer/id #uuid "34ee353c-18cb-58d7-b73d-63478f3748f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c836109-2926-5149-86de-cfbf47a6956c" :answer/id #uuid "2c836109-2926-5149-86de-cfbf47a6956c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8214dd72-d6d8-590d-81b7-fddc4ba96e98" :answer/id #uuid "8214dd72-d6d8-590d-81b7-fddc4ba96e98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa30ad19-820a-5c33-a201-b90a4102fe1c" :answer/id #uuid "fa30ad19-820a-5c33-a201-b90a4102fe1c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30f9ac07-0f6b-5c34-a6ab-9f12e00a0258" :answer/id #uuid "30f9ac07-0f6b-5c34-a6ab-9f12e00a0258" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172617 :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The amount of time until the next earthquake in a particular country\n\nII. The number of giraffes in a zoo\n\nIII. The number of defective bottles produced in a factory in a single day" :question/worked-solution "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Earthquakes can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next earthquake occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of continuous time or space.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of defective bottles produced is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171327 :answer-field/correct 17592186171330 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f0c3a40-75d0-54f9-9bbc-0aacfac8fedd" :question/id #uuid "4f0c3a40-75d0-54f9-9bbc-0aacfac8fedd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132551" :question/problem "The lifetime of a certain type of electrical switch varies continuously. Lifetime of different switches of this type are independent, and switches replacements have a constant average rate of one every $2$ years. Determine the probability that a switch fails between the first and the second year. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since the lifetime of the switches varies continuously, lifetimes of different switches are independent, and switches replacements have a constant average rate, we can model the lifetime of a switch using the exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the lifetime, in years, of a random electrical switch. The lifetime can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since switches are replaced every $2$ years on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{2}).\n$$\n\nIn this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a switch fails between the first and the second year is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1<X<2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/2}{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.2387\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cac80f7e-d672-5cc4-9408-ca831ac21b23"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cac80f7e-d672-5cc4-9408-ca831ac21b23" :answer-field/id #uuid "cac80f7e-d672-5cc4-9408-ca831ac21b23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5d327df7-7c16-534b-b4bd-6d64d75f1a58" :answer-field/choices ["answer-147b0fac-812b-5ffc-b68e-0bb7f6a18d22" "answer-2c71bce0-cdf7-58fe-9bdc-53f8437b56f8" "answer-5d327df7-7c16-534b-b4bd-6d64d75f1a58" "answer-4786f06d-9649-56ec-9062-82028da85807" "answer-fcd4a6c0-5472-5657-8ceb-ca87405f68b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-147b0fac-812b-5ffc-b68e-0bb7f6a18d22" :answer/id #uuid "147b0fac-812b-5ffc-b68e-0bb7f6a18d22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2337" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c71bce0-cdf7-58fe-9bdc-53f8437b56f8" :answer/id #uuid "2c71bce0-cdf7-58fe-9bdc-53f8437b56f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2378" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d327df7-7c16-534b-b4bd-6d64d75f1a58" :answer/id #uuid "5d327df7-7c16-534b-b4bd-6d64d75f1a58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2387" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4786f06d-9649-56ec-9062-82028da85807" :answer/id #uuid "4786f06d-9649-56ec-9062-82028da85807" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2837" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcd4a6c0-5472-5657-8ceb-ca87405f68b5" :answer/id #uuid "fcd4a6c0-5472-5657-8ceb-ca87405f68b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2873" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172635 :question/worked-solution "Since the errors occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next error using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next error. The time until the next error can be modeled as an exponential random variable. Since errors occur once every $30$ days on average, the rate is $\\lambda =1/30,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{30}).\n$$\n\n In this case, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that first error will be registered between $2$ and $12$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<12) & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{12}\\frac{1}{30}{e}^{-x/30}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/30}{|}_{2}^{12} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2/5}+{e}^{-1/15} \\\\  & ≈0.2652\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171435 :answer-field/correct 17592186171438 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-2327e247-df72-5db4-a448-f8b66a015dc8" :question/id #uuid "2327e247-df72-5db4-a448-f8b66a015dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-132557" :question/problem "The arrivals of vehicles at a crossing occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ seconds. Determine the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing at least $5$ seconds after the previous vehicle's arrival. Round your answer to four decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Since arrivals of vehicles at the crossing occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next vehicle arrives as an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time between the arrival of two successive vehicles at the crossing. Then, since vehicles arrive every $10$ seconds on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ vehicles per second.\n\nThe time between arrivals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next vehicle will arrive at the crossing at least $5$ seconds after the previous vehicle's arrival is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/10}{|}_{5}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-5/10} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.6065.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-24dc1c8a-fb67-5e74-8896-7a038fa53b08"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-24dc1c8a-fb67-5e74-8896-7a038fa53b08" :answer-field/id #uuid "24dc1c8a-fb67-5e74-8896-7a038fa53b08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-580b5684-e419-5b9e-b864-fa01906c4359" :answer-field/choices ["answer-580b5684-e419-5b9e-b864-fa01906c4359" "answer-dfd0df45-322b-55b0-9128-385e1d5512b3" "answer-cf3cc786-c668-51b8-b59b-12ba113e23a3" "answer-89bc60de-fb5a-54b4-85ac-135fedd57202" "answer-2601e9a7-969d-56ed-a92d-f155b2b06308"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-580b5684-e419-5b9e-b864-fa01906c4359" :answer/id #uuid "580b5684-e419-5b9e-b864-fa01906c4359" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6065" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfd0df45-322b-55b0-9128-385e1d5512b3" :answer/id #uuid "dfd0df45-322b-55b0-9128-385e1d5512b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6105" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf3cc786-c668-51b8-b59b-12ba113e23a3" :answer/id #uuid "cf3cc786-c668-51b8-b59b-12ba113e23a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6565" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89bc60de-fb5a-54b4-85ac-135fedd57202" :answer/id #uuid "89bc60de-fb5a-54b4-85ac-135fedd57202" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6605" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2601e9a7-969d-56ed-a92d-f155b2b06308" :answer/id #uuid "2601e9a7-969d-56ed-a92d-f155b2b06308" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6665" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172619 :question/worked-solution "Since the earthquakes occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next earthquake using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time until the next earthquake. Since there is an earthquake every $2.5$ months on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2.5}=2/5$ earthquakes per month.\n\nThe number of earthquakes can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =2/5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{2}{5}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next earthquake will not occur for at least $6$ months is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{-2x/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-2x/5}|}_{6}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-{e}^{-\\infty }+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & =0+{e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ={e}^{-12/5} \\\\  & ≈0.0907.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171339 :answer-field/correct 17592186171340 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0991a4b-acbc-5194-a13a-e698ad64e9cb" :question/id #uuid "b0991a4b-acbc-5194-a13a-e698ad64e9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-254607" :question/problem "Emails arrive in a professor's inbox continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $2$ emails per day. If the professor has not received an email for the past $3$ days, what is the probability that an email will arrive in the next $2$ days? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since emails arrive continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next email using the exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ represent the time, in days, until the next email arrives. Given that emails arrive at an average rate of $2$ per day, the rate parameter is $\\lambda =2$ emails per day, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2,$ $s=3,$ and $t=2.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3+2\\,|\\,X>3) & =P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(2)(2)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-4} \\\\  & ≈0.982.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72ed1eca-9fc4-5b18-a717-801450da7248"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72ed1eca-9fc4-5b18-a717-801450da7248" :answer-field/id #uuid "72ed1eca-9fc4-5b18-a717-801450da7248" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-105e844e-764d-56e7-b3ce-44d005a43128" :answer-field/choices ["answer-105e844e-764d-56e7-b3ce-44d005a43128"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-105e844e-764d-56e7-b3ce-44d005a43128" :answer/id #uuid "105e844e-764d-56e7-b3ce-44d005a43128" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.982" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2e0173ce-d070-51b0-81c2-1bf7c8171fcf" :question/id #uuid "2e0173ce-d070-51b0-81c2-1bf7c8171fcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-254604" :question/problem "Calls to a customer service center occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one call every $10$ minutes. If no calls have been received for $20$ minutes, what is the probability that the next call will come within the next $5$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since calls occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call using the exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the time until the next call in minutes. Since calls occur every $10$ minutes on average, the rate is  $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ calls per minute, and we can write\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. Therefore, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the complementary conditional probability as follows:\n\n$$\nP(X\\le s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-P(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=1-{e}^{-\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ $s=20,$ and $t=5.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20+5\\,|\\,X>20) & =P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-(1/10)\\cdot (5)} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-1/2} \\\\  & ≈0.393.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4df0aa64-eae3-5c33-8bf5-0feea3f9c60f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4df0aa64-eae3-5c33-8bf5-0feea3f9c60f" :answer-field/id #uuid "4df0aa64-eae3-5c33-8bf5-0feea3f9c60f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1ef2502c-ce4b-503e-bf43-b04378df513f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1ef2502c-ce4b-503e-bf43-b04378df513f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ef2502c-ce4b-503e-bf43-b04378df513f" :answer/id #uuid "1ef2502c-ce4b-503e-bf43-b04378df513f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.393" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]}]
